\section{Problem Modeling}

Consider a LEO mega-constellation where each satellite is equipped with optical inter-satellite link (ISL) terminals. The establishment of an optical link between any two satellites requires the simultaneous satisfaction of multiple geometric and terminal-specific constraints. These constraints arise from physical limitations of the optical terminals and the orbital mechanics of the constellation.

The feasibility of an optical ISL depends on: (i) the line-of-sight geometry between satellites; (ii) the slant range and free-space propagation characteristics; (iii) the beam-pointing dynamics and angular velocity tracking capabilities; (iv) the mechanical field-of-regard of the terminal's coarse pointing assembly; and (v) the minimum acquisition time required to establish and maintain the link. Below we formalize each constraint and the conditions under which an optical ISL may be established.

\subsection{Line-of-Sight (LoS) Constraint}

Optical signals cannot propagate through Earth. Thus, an unobstructed line-of-sight path between two satellites is a necessary condition. Let $\mathbf{r}_i, \mathbf{r}_j$ denote the position vectors of satellites $i$ and $j$ in an Earth-centered inertial frame, measured in kilometers. The line segment connecting them, $[\mathbf{r}_i, \mathbf{r}_j]$, must not intersect the Earth's exclusion zone, defined as the sphere of radius $R_e + \delta_{\text{atm}}$, where $R_e$ is the Earth's equatorial radius and $\delta_{\text{atm}}$ is an atmospheric and refraction margin.

The condition is satisfied if the minimum distance from Earth's center to the line segment exceeds this radius:

\begin{equation}
\min_{\mathbf{r} \in [\mathbf{r}_i,\mathbf{r}_j]} \|\mathbf{r}\| - R_e \ge \delta_{\text{atm}} \label{eq:los_altitude}
\end{equation}

In practice, this minimum is computed as the perpendicular distance from the origin to the chord, minus Earth's radius. A typical value is $\delta_{\text{atm}} = 80$ km.

\subsection{Maximum Inter-Satellite Range Constraint}

The optical signal strength received at the destination decreases with distance via free-space path loss. Thus, the slant range between two satellites is bounded by the maximum distance at which the link budget remains positive. Let $d_{ij} = \|\mathbf{r}_j - \mathbf{r}_i\|$ denote the slant range. The link is feasible only if:

\begin{equation}
d_{ij} \le d_{\max} \label{eq:range_constraint}
\end{equation}

The threshold $d_{\max}$ depends on the terminal's transmission power, receiver sensitivity, modulation scheme, and required data rate. Typical values for optical terminals operating at multi-Gbps rates fall in the range 5000–8000 km.

\subsection{Angular Velocity Constraint}

Optical beam pointing and tracking requires that the beam-steering gimbal remain capable of following the target. As the target moves across the sky (as seen by the observer), the rate of angular change is bounded by the mechanical slew-rate capability of the coarse pointing assembly (CPA).

\subsubsection{Formulation}

Let $\phi_{ij}(t)$ and $\theta_{ij}(t)$ denote the azimuth and elevation angles of the line-of-sight direction from satellite $i$ to satellite $j$, expressed in the Local Vertical Local Horizontal (LVLH) reference frame of satellite $i$. The constraint on angular velocity is:

\begin{equation}
\sqrt{\dot{\phi}_{ij}^2(t) + \dot{\theta}_{ij}^2(t)} \le \omega_{\max}(t) \label{eq:total_angular_velocity}
\end{equation}

where $\omega_{\max}$ is the maximum allowed total angular slew rate and the rates are estimated via finite differences:

\begin{equation}
\dot{\phi}_{ij}(t) \approx \frac{\phi_{ij}(t + \Delta t) - \phi_{ij}(t)}{\Delta t}, \quad
\dot{\theta}_{ij}(t) \approx \frac{\theta_{ij}(t + \Delta t) - \theta_{ij}(t)}{\Delta t}
\label{eq:angular_rate_fd}
\end{equation}

over a small prediction interval $\Delta t$ derived from the satellite's ephemeris.

\subsection{Field-of-Regard (CPA) Constraint}

Each optical terminal is mounted at a fixed boresight direction relative to the satellite body frame. The optical beam may only be steered within a limited angular region around this boresight direction, determined by the mechanical gimbal design and the terminal's optical aperture.

\subsubsection{General Formulation}

Let $\phi_b^i, \theta_b^i$ denote the boresight azimuth and elevation of terminal $i$ in the LVLH frame. Let $\phi_{ij}, \theta_{ij}$ be the angles to the target satellite $j$. The deviation of the target from the boresight must satisfy:

\begin{equation}
\left| \left( \phi_{ij} - \phi_b^i + 180° \right) \bmod 360° - 180° \right| \le \phi_{\max}, \quad
\theta_{\min} \le \theta_{ij} - \theta_b^i \le \theta_{\max} \label{eq:for_combined}
\end{equation}

where $\phi_{\max}$ is the azimuth half-width (typically $\approx 175°$), and $[\theta_{\min}, \theta_{\max}]$ is the elevation range relative to boresight. The asymmetry in elevation (e.g., $\theta_{\min} = -25°, \theta_{\max} = +5°$) reflects the orbital geometry; intra-plane links typically appear below the local horizontal due to orbital curvature.

\subsection{Minimum Contact Duration Constraint}

Not all geometrically feasible link configurations are practically useful. Link acquisition—the initial pointing, tracking lock, and optical signal detection—requires finite time. A link is only valuable if it remains feasible for at least a minimum duration $T_{\min}$, typically on the order of tens of seconds.

\subsubsection{Duration Estimation}

The contact duration is estimated by projecting the rate of change of each constraint margin and determining when the first constraint will be violated. For each constraint $c$, let $M_c(t)$ denote the margin (remaining slack) relative to its threshold, and $\dot{M}_c(t)$ the rate of change of that margin. The time until constraint $c$ is first violated is:

\begin{equation}
t_c = \frac{M_c(t)}{|\dot{M}_c(t)|} \quad \text{if } M_c > 0 \text{ and } \dot{M}_c < 0 \label{eq:margin_time}
\end{equation}

The predicted contact duration is then:

\begin{equation}
T_{\text{contact}} = \min\{t_c : c \in C\} \label{eq:contact_duration}
\end{equation}

where $C = \{\text{el, az, range, } \omega, \text{ los}\}$ is the set of active constraints.

\subsubsection{Angular Velocity Time-to-Violation}

For the angular velocity constraint, the rate of angular growth depends nonlinearly on the range as satellites approach. A conservative geometric estimate replaces the instantaneous rate with a range-dependent model:

\begin{equation}
r_{\text{crit}} = \frac{\omega(t) \cdot r(t)}{\omega_{\max}}, \quad
t_\omega = \frac{r(t) - r_{\text{crit}}}{-\dot{r}(t)}
\label{eq:tvelocity_omega}
\end{equation}

This accounts for the acceleration of angular velocity during approach without requiring integration of the full equations of motion.

\subsubsection{Line-of-Sight Time-to-Violation}

The LoS altitude $h(t) = \min_{\mathbf{r} \in [\mathbf{r}_i,\mathbf{r}_j]} \|\mathbf{r}\| - R_e$ may decrease over time, causing LoS to be lost. The time-to-violation is:

\begin{equation}
t_{\text{los}} = \frac{h(t) - \delta_{\text{atm}}}{-\dot{h}(t)} \quad \text{if } \dot{h} < 0, \quad t_{\text{los}} = \infty \text{ otherwise}
\label{eq:tvelocity_los}
\end{equation}

The feasibility check then requires $T_{\text{contact}} \ge T_{\min}$.

\subsection{Bidirectional Link Requirement}

The nature of optical communication imposes a symmetry requirement: a useful inter-satellite link must be feasible in both directions simultaneously. An inter-satellite link is established only if all constraints are satisfied in both directions:

\begin{equation}
\text{Link}_{i \leftrightarrow j} = \text{Link}_{i \to j} \land \text{Link}_{j \to i} \label{eq:bidirectional}
\end{equation}

This arises both from the practical need for bidirectional data exchange and from the constraint geometry itself: the LoS altitude and range are symmetric between two points, while azimuth, elevation, and angular velocity constraints are terminal-dependent and must be verified at both ends.

\subsection{Composite Link Feasibility}

The feasibility of an optical inter-satellite link from terminal $a$ (on satellite $i$) to terminal $b$ (on satellite $j$) is determined by the logical AND of seven necessary conditions:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{Link}_{\text{feasible}} = & \, \text{LoS}_{ij} \land \text{Range}_{ij} \land \text{Az-FoR}_{a} \land \text{El-FoR}_{a} \\
& \land \text{Az-FoR}_{b} \land \text{El-FoR}_{b} \land \omega_{ij} \land T_{\text{contact}} \ge T_{\min}
\label{eq:feasibility_composite}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

Each constraint function depends on the satellite ephemeris, terminal parameters ($d_{\max}, \omega_{\max}, \phi_{\max}, [\theta_{\min}, \theta_{\max}], \phi_b, \theta_b, T_{\min}$), and orbital velocities. The binary feasibility is supplemented by continuous-valued metrics such as alignment score, estimated contact duration, and margin profiles, which enable ranking and selection among candidate links.
